==English==

===Etymology===
{{etyl|ang|en}} {{term/t|ang|dēadlīċ}} (adj.), {{term/t|ang|dēadlīċe}} (adv.), corresponding to {{suffix|dead|ly}}. Cognate with Dutch {{term|dodelijk|lang=nl}}, German {{term|tödlich|lang=de}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈdɛdli/}}
* {{audio|en-us-deadly.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|er|more}}

# {{label|en|obsolete}} Subject to [[death]]; [[mortal]].
#* '''1485''', Sir {{w|Thomas Malory}}, ''{{w|Le Morte d'Arthur}}'', Book XVII:
#*: And than he began to tremble ryght harde whan the '''dedly''' fleysh began to beholde the spirituall thynges.
#* Wyclif Bible, {{w|Epistle to the Romans|Romans}} i. 23
#*: The image of a '''deadly''' man.
# Causing death; [[lethal]].
# Aiming or willing to destroy; implacable; desperately hostile.
#: {{usex|lang=en|'''deadly''' enemies}}
#* {{w|William Shakespeare}} (1564-1616)
#*: Thy assailant is quick, skillful, and '''deadly'''.
# {{label|en|by extension}} Very [[accurate]] (of aiming with a bow, firearm, etc.).
#*{{quote-book|year=1879|author={{w|Richard Jefferies}}|title=[http://gutenberg.org/ebooks/13730 The Amateur Poacher]|chapter=1
|passage=But then I had the [massive] flintlock by me for protection. ¶{{...}}The linen-press and a chest on the top of it formed, however, a very good gun-carriage; and, thus mounted, aim could be taken out of the window&nbsp;[&hellip;], and a 'bead' could be drawn upon Molly, the dairymaid, kissing the fogger behind the hedge, little dreaming that the '''deadly''' tube was levelled at them.}}
# {{label|en|informal}} Very [[boring]].
#*{{quote-book|year=1907|author=[[w:Robert W. Chambers|Robert Chambers]]
|chapter=6|title=[http://openlibrary.org/works/OL8127246W The Younger Set]
|passage=“I don't mean all of your friends—only a small proportion—which, however, connects your circle with that '''deadly''', idle, brainless bunch—the insolent chatterers at the opera, the gorged dowagers, … !”}}
# {{label|en|informal}} [[excellent|Excellent]], [[awesome]], [[cool]].

